by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-02T08:36:26Z

Thanks Amoifr, the Bcc handling and the packaging are right now. Rebased on 8.2, with four changes before merging.

The envelope is authoritative rather than additive. `getEnvelopeOnlyRecipients()` only added addresses, so an envelope that narrows the recipients was ignored and the message still reached the original ones. That is the staging redirect shape, `framework.mailer.envelope.recipients`, where being ignored does real damage: the envelope said `staging@symfony.com`, the raw still carried `To: real-customer@example.com` and `Cc: real-boss@example.com`, and Gmail delivered to both. `applyEnvelope()` now removes from `To` and `Cc` what the envelope drops and sends what it adds through `Bcc`. An envelope sender still cannot be expressed, the API has no field for it, and the README says so.

The message sent and the message reported no longer disagree. Rebuilding the payload from `getPreparedHeaders()` generated a second `Message-ID`, because that method works on a clone and mints one when the email has none, so the id you logged was not the id you sent. `SentMessage` has already settled on an id by then, so the raw reuses it.

Both x86 jobs were red, and only on this bridge: deleting `TokenManagerMock` moved RSA key generation into a data provider, and those runners cannot do it (`openssl_pkey_export(): Cannot get key from parameter 1`, then `The data provider ... is invalid`). There is a committed key at `Tests/Fixtures/private_key.pem` now, and no test generates one any more.

`TokenManager` is no longer `@internal`: it is the first constructor argument of a public final transport, so it cannot be built without it.

Two new tests cover the first two points and both fail when the fix is reverted. Left as follow-ups: the duplication with the MicrosoftGraph `TokenManager`, and the private key being visible through `print_r()` on the manager.
